WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:10.194
"The Best Exercise Type
and Frequency for Bone Density"

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.680
When it comes to bone health,
it's use it or lose it.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.220
Physical activity is considered
a widely accessible, low-cost,

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.920
and highly modifiable
contributor to bone health.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.640
Exercise transmits forces
through the skeleton,

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.380
generating signals
that are detected

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.700
by your bone-building cells.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.920
This is why the National
Osteoporosis Foundation,

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.660
International Osteoporosis
Foundation,

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.180
and other agencies recommend
weight-bearing exercise

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.640
for the prevention
of osteoporosis.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.860
These include
high-impact exercises

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.320
such as jumping, aerobics,
and running,

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.720
as well as
lower impact exercises

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.160
like walking
and weight training

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.020
to create
those mechanical signals

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.400
that spark bone growth,

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.780
but sufficient intensity
and frequency are critical.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.480
The large variation
in bone benefit

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.620
across different studies,

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.560
from negligible changes
to substantial improvements

00:00:58.560 --> 00:00:59.580
in bone mineral density,

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.620
have been attributed
to the adequacy

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.180
of the exercise regime.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.520
To improve measures
of bone strength

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:06.360
at the spine and hip,

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.760
the most effective
exercise training protocol

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.020
appears to be a combination

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.420
of progressive resistance
and impact training

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:13.920
at moderate to high intensity.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.640
Low-intensity exercise does not
appear to be sufficient.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.740
For example,
while regular walking

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.440
is often prescribed
to prevent osteoporosis,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.720
it appears to offer
limited benefit

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.100
for bone loss prevention.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.280
On its own, walking
has no significant effect

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.260
on bone mineral density
in the spine, wrist,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.140
or overall skeleton,
but it has been shown

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.960
to significantly improve
hip bone density

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.180
in studies that have lasted
more than six months.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.820
More effective would be
brisk walking,

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.920
walking with a weighted vest,

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.560
or combining walking
with more vigorous exercises

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.140
such as jogging, stepping,
or stair-climbing.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.200
Non-impact activities
such as cycling or swimming

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.420
have been shown
to have little or no effect.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.920
An elegant study to determine
the optimum frequency

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.080
of high-impact exercise
for bone health

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.020
involved hopping
on one randomly chosen foot,

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.900
with the person's other leg
acting as the control.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.840
Women were randomized
to hop 50 times

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.020
on that one, same leg
either seven days a week,

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.060
four days a week,
two days a week,

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.160
or not at all for six months.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.820
And the brief, daily hopping
increased hip bone density,

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.460
but less frequent hopping
was not effective.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.680
The only group that built

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.960
significantly more bone
in their hip on the jumping

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.300
versus non-jumping side
within those six months

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.160
was the seven-day-a-week group.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.080
If you jump 50 times

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.620
with about a 10-pound (4.5 kg)
weighted vest on, however,

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.080
you may be able to preserve
your hip bone density

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.720
with just three sessions a week
instead of every day.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.080
Note, weight-bearing
impact exercise

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.140
may be contraindicated,
meaning not advisable,

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.300
in those
with severe osteoporosis

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:52.800
or recent history of fracture,

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.020
so make sure you check
with your medical professional

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:56.400
before you get going.

